¶
{{smaller block|There was Wollaston, a portrait painter, who could only command five guineas for a ''three-quarters'' canvas.
{{right|''J. Ashton'', Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, II. 42.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Three-quarter binding.''' See ''binding.''— '''Three-quarter fiddle''' or '''violin.''' See ''violin.''}}

'''three-quartered''' (thrē′kwâr′tėrd), ''a.'' In ''her.'', turned so as to be nearly affronté, but showing a part of the flank: noting an animal used as a bearing.

'''three-ribbed''' (thrē′ribd), ''a.'' In ''bot.'', having three ribs; tricostate: as, a ''three-ribbed'' leaf.

'''threescore''' (thrē′skōr), ''a.'' [< ''three'' + ''score''<sup>1</sup>.] Thrice twenty; sixty: as, ''threescore'' years: often used without its noun.

{{smaller block|''Threescore'' and ten I can remember well.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 4. 1.}}
One man has reach't his sixty yeers, but he
Of all those ''three-score'' has not liv'd halfe three.
{{right|''Herrick'', On Himself.}}
The brave soldier had already numbered, nearly or quite, his ''threescore'' years and ten.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 21.}}}}

'''threesome''' (thrē′sum), ''a.'' [< ''three'' + ''-some.''] Triple; danced by three persons. [Scotch.]

{{smaller block|There 's ''threesome'' reels, there 's foursome reels,
There 's hornpipes and strathspeys, man.
{{right|''Burns'', The Exciseman.}}}}

'''three-square''' (thrē′skwãr), ''a.'' See ''square''<sup>1</sup>.

'''three-suited''' (thrē′sū″ted), ''a.'' Having only three suits of clothes, or wearing three suits of clothes (referring to a custom, once prevalent among the peasantry of Germany, of putting on their whole wardrobe on festival occasions, one suit over another). [Rare.]

{{smaller block|A knave; a rascal; an eater of broken meats; a base,}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|proud, shallow, beggarly, ''three-suited'', hundred-pound, filthy, worsted-stocking knave. {{right|''Shak.'', Lear, ii. 2. 16.}}}}

'''three-thirds†''', ''n.'' See ''three thirds'', under ''three''.

'''three-thorned''' (thrē′thôrnd), ''a.'' Having three thorns or a triple thorn.—'''Three-thorned acacia''', {{smaller|the honey-locust, ''Gleditschia triacanthos'': so called from its savage triple or still more compound thorn.}}

'''three-valved''' (thrē′valvd), ''a.'' In ''bot.'', having, or opening by, three valves.

'''three-way''' (thrē′wā), ''a.'' Having or governing three openings or passages: generally noting a special form of pipe-connection, valve, stopcock, etc.—'''Three-way place''', {{smaller|in ''ornith.'', an extravestibular chamber of the inner ear, at the point where the three semicircular canals have a cavity in common. ''Coues'', Key to N. A. Birds, p. 190.}}

'''threisshfold†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''threshold''.

'''thremmatology''' (threm-ạ-tol′ọ̄-ji), ''n.'' [< Gr. θρέμμα(τ-), a nursling (< τρέφειν, nourish), + -λογία, < λέγειν, speak: see ''-ology''.] In ''biol.'', the science of breeding or propagating animals and plants under domestication, of their congenital variations under these circumstances, and of the perpetuation of such variations. See ''methodical selection'', under ''selection''.

{{smaller block|Darwin's introduction of ''thremmatology'' into the domain of scientific biology was accompanied by a new and special development of a branch of study which had previously been known as teleology. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XXIV. 802.}}}}

'''threne''' (thrēn), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''threane''; < L. ''threnus'', < Gr. θρῆνος, lamentation, < θρεῖσθαι, cry aloud.] A threnody; also, lamentation. [Obsolete or archaic.]

