or by treading with oxen: in this sense commonly ''thresh.''

{{smaller block|And zuo hit is of the hyeape of huete ''y-thorsse.'' The cornes byeth benethe and thet chef a-boue.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 139.}}}}

{{smaller block|And his son Gideon ''threshed'' wheat by the winepress, to hide it from the Midianites.
{{right|Judges vi. 11.}}}}

{{smaller block|First ''thrash'' the corn, then after burn the straw.
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., ii. 3. 123.}}}}

2. To beat soundly, as with a stick or whip; drub; hence, to beat in any way: in this sense commonly ''thrash.'' [Now colloq.]

{{smaller block|Full many wounds in his corrupted flesh
He did engrave; . . . but aie more fresh
And fierce he still appeard, the more he did him ''thresh.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. vii. 32.}}}}

{{smaller block|I could find a man of a smaller scale
Could ''thrash'' the pedlar and also thee.
{{right|''Bold Pedlar and Robin Hood'' (Child's Ballads, V. 250).}}}}

{{smaller block|Do you remember his fight with Ringwood? What an infernal bully he was, and how glad we all were when Brackley ''thrashed'' him!
{{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, xl.}}}}

II. ''intrans.'' 1. To practise threshing; beat out grain from straw with a flail or a threshing-machine: in this sense commonly ''thresh.''

{{smaller block|Some tyme I sowe and some tyme I ''thresche.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), v. 553.}}}}

2. To beat about; labor; drudge; toil.

{{smaller block|I rather would be Mævius, ''thrash'' for rhymes
Like his, the scorn and scandal of the times.
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Juvenal's Satires, x. 194.}}}}

3. To throw one's self about; toss to and fro: usually with ''about:'' in this sense commonly ''thrash.''

